{:edb/db-before-t 845 :edb/db-after-t 846 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534158 50 #inst "2026-10-06T06:01:46.646Z" 13194139534158 true] [17592186059594 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279416 13194139534158 true] [17592186060108 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534158 true] [17592186060108 1100 "Let $a = ⟨ - 1, 3, 2⟩$ and $b = ⟨5, 1, 4⟩$. Find $3a + b$." 13194139534158 false] [17592186060108 1100 "Let $a=⟨-1,3,2⟩$ and $b=⟨5,1,4⟩.$ Find $3a+b.$" 13194139534158 true] [17592186060108 1145 "To compute $3a+b,$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+b & =3\\cdot ⟨-1,3,2⟩+⟨5,1,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,9,6⟩+⟨5,1,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,10,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534158 true] [17592186064065 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279395 13194139534158 true] [17592186068620 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279430 13194139534158 true] [17592186068707 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279437 13194139534158 true] [17592186068789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279402 13194139534158 true] [17592186087761 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279409 13194139534158 true] [17592186097188 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534158 true] [17592186097188 1100 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{4}3\\sqrt{x}\\,dx$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" 13194139534158 false] [17592186097188 1100 "What is ${\\int }_{2}^{4}3\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" 13194139534158 true] [17592186097188 1145 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{4}3\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=3\\sqrt{x},$ the lower bound is $a=2,$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-2}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}3\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(2+j\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(2+\\frac{2j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[3\\sqrt{2+\\frac{2j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534158 true] [17592186129077 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279423 13194139534158 true] [17592186279395 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279396 13194139534158 true] [17592186279395 1089 "q-112063" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279395 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534158 true] [17592186279395 1100 "Consider an ellipse such that its major axis $a$ can vary while its minor axis is fixed. What is the best interpretation of ${S}^{′}(a)$ if $S$ is the area enclosed by the ellipse?" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279395 1113 #uuid "17829a12-d8a2-500a-8faf-3df160b9f2ac" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279395 1145 "The function $S(a)$ gives the area of the ellipse with major axis $a.$ So,\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(a)=\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}a}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }S}{\\text{Change in }a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${S}^{′}(a)$ is the rate at which the area changes as the major axis changes." 13194139534158 true] [17592186279396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279397 13194139534158 true] [17592186279396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279398 13194139534158 true] [17592186279396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279399 13194139534158 true] [17592186279396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279400 13194139534158 true] [17592186279396 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279401 13194139534158 true] [17592186279396 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534158 true] [17592186279396 1041 "selection" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279396 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279401 13194139534158 true] [17592186279396 1110 #uuid "172ec8c9-c6fe-505a-aa6a-3c80be1bf22e" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279397 1034 "The rate at which the major axis changes as the minor axis changes" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279397 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534158 true] [17592186279397 1111 #uuid "f62c62f2-f8fa-5e49-8c23-99b3f1aeac51" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279398 1034 "The major axis of an ellipse with area S" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279398 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534158 true] [17592186279398 1111 #uuid "c08805e6-1136-57ee-acbb-1fd8923ea685" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279399 1034 "The area of an ellipse with major axis $a$" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279399 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534158 true] [17592186279399 1111 #uuid "f574a895-e26d-5f01-aaf0-c76a3f6c0a3a" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279400 1034 "The rate at which the perimeter changes as the major axis changes" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279400 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534158 true] [17592186279400 1111 #uuid "c375f04e-2638-5d1e-af8e-e243076948f8" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279401 1034 "The rate at which the area changes as the major axis changes" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279401 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534158 true] [17592186279401 1111 #uuid "62dd8bfc-4e63-5be9-abbd-1ca48ab30785" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279402 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279403 13194139534158 true] [17592186279402 1089 "q-12980" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279402 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534158 true] [17592186279402 1100 "The equation ${a}^{2}+8a=18$ can be written in the form $(a+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139534158 true] [17592186279402 1113 #uuid "614f3d78-9dad-57cb-a541-b612d5595aca" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279402 1145 "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+8a & =18 \\\\ {a}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot a & =18 \\\\ {a}^{2}+2\\cdot a\\cdot 4+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =18 \\\\ (a+4)^{2}-{4}^{2} & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+4)^{2}-{4}^{2} & =18 \\\\ (a+4)^{2}-16 & =18 \\\\ (a+4)^{2} & =34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=4,q=34,$ and $p+q=38.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+4)^{2} & =34 \\\\ \\sqrt{(a+4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{34} \\\\ |a+4| & =\\sqrt{34} \\\\ a+4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{34} \\\\ a & =-4\\pm \\sqrt{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279403 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279404 13194139534158 true] [17592186279403 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279405 13194139534158 true] [17592186279403 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279406 13194139534158 true] [17592186279403 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279407 13194139534158 true] [17592186279403 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279408 13194139534158 true] [17592186279403 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534158 true] [17592186279403 1041 "selection" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279403 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279404 13194139534158 true] [17592186279403 1110 #uuid "b844afc2-b829-5d87-b161-5cbbd847b061" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279404 1034 "p+q=38,\\,a=-4\\pm \\sqrt{34}" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279404 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534158 true] [17592186279404 1111 #uuid "80f9a2c4-ac98-5267-90b0-5c84f634a74b" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279405 1034 "p+q=38,\\,a=4+\\sqrt{34}" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279405 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534158 true] [17592186279405 1111 #uuid "558dff4f-a12b-5f7f-a225-5bad49cfcb1d" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279406 1034 "p+q=35,\\,a=-4-\\sqrt{34}" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279406 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534158 true] [17592186279406 1111 #uuid "a5500bcf-c7c3-5206-91f0-09dd77f547c6" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279407 1034 "p+q=-38,\\,a=-4\\pm \\sqrt{35}" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279407 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534158 true] [17592186279407 1111 #uuid "54e29105-bd7b-5261-a1ef-7615fbb055a1" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279408 1034 "p+q=-35,\\,a=-4\\pm \\sqrt{34}" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279408 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534158 true] [17592186279408 1111 #uuid "e8a46fc1-1750-5ffa-988a-d5755e009b22" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279409 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279410 13194139534158 true] [17592186279409 1089 "q-170593" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279409 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534158 true] [17592186279409 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/319245f467f8512e0fd1d420364a3a88774eda3c9c17c03d4cba6b073c86ad9e.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." 13194139534158 true] [17592186279409 1113 #uuid "4441553d-da09-5372-be95-e3b73397a93b" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279409 1145 "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ The vertical translation $y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, $c=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279410 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279411 13194139534158 true] [17592186279410 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279412 13194139534158 true] [17592186279410 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279413 13194139534158 true] [17592186279410 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279414 13194139534158 true] [17592186279410 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279415 13194139534158 true] [17592186279410 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534158 true] [17592186279410 1041 "selection" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279410 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279412 13194139534158 true] [17592186279410 1110 #uuid "54121805-0fcb-5ba5-84b7-7c5ecb6f3679" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279411 1034 "y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}-\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279411 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534158 true] [17592186279411 1111 #uuid "4ba07c5d-8f98-5465-9e59-74764fa399b0" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279412 1034 "y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}-\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279412 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534158 true] [17592186279412 1111 #uuid "a5723b39-7d72-5932-b15a-4b2403e3a2f4" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279413 1034 "y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279413 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534158 true] [17592186279413 1111 #uuid "817f8320-b41b-548d-8f19-7b9cefd81cdd" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279414 1034 "y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}+\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279414 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534158 true] [17592186279414 1111 #uuid "4b779fdd-dacc-5b89-9823-db77638e93d9" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279415 1034 "y={(\\frac{13}{6})}^{x}+\\frac{5}{6}" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279415 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534158 true] [17592186279415 1111 #uuid "a46266a8-da36-583b-aef7-b97cc27f1aa1" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279416 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279417 13194139534158 true] [17592186279416 1089 "q-211467" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279416 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534158 true] [17592186279416 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978982/assets/d8d23cf36bc9fb526c0e6d041b72e6b378638f662b67aae63531c66b4a8cd6c5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=-f(x+2)+4?$" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279416 1113 #uuid "75c8a190-b092-5b03-8511-92274bf3e6ef" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279416 1145 "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(x+2)+4$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the left, then\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $4$ units up.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **maximum** of $y=-f(x+2)+4.$ So, we need to find the image of $(1,-4),$ the local minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(x+2)+4,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(1,-4).$\n\n- Then, we translate the graph by $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1-2,-4)=(-1,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-1\\cdot (-4))=(-1,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $4$ units up and get the graph of $y=-f(x+2)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,4+4)=(-1,8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=-f(x+2)+4$ is $(-1,8).$" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279417 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279418 13194139534158 true] [17592186279417 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279419 13194139534158 true] [17592186279417 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279420 13194139534158 true] [17592186279417 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279421 13194139534158 true] [17592186279417 1000 #edb/ref 17592186279422 13194139534158 true] [17592186279417 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534158 true] [17592186279417 1041 "selection" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279417 1104 #edb/ref 17592186279422 13194139534158 true] [17592186279417 1110 #uuid "b5fd171c-13e2-5c92-8a60-5a006bd259bb" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279418 1034 "(3,8)" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279418 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534158 true] [17592186279418 1111 #uuid "ca2e8fe1-b567-518a-afc6-3168329e721c" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279419 1034 "(2,5)" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279419 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534158 true] [17592186279419 1111 #uuid "c6ae1596-526e-508d-93b5-68553167d632" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279420 1034 "(2,0)" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279420 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534158 true] [17592186279420 1111 #uuid "f59f2465-d3d0-5eb3-b992-ce8b3ed21929" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279421 1034 "(-1,0)" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279421 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534158 true] [17592186279421 1111 #uuid "089092dc-a832-5d38-9cdf-4903a4a3d120" 13194139534158 true] [17592186279422 103